<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Pampa Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, with its capital at Santa Rosa, covering part of the pampas plains and the drier lands to the west. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> of 1878–1879 it was largely held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranquel">Ranquel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> peoples. It was organised as a national territory in 1884 and became a province in 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-pampa-province.jpg" alt="La Pampa Province" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hardscarf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parque_Nacional_Lihu%C3%A9_Calel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> is a sparsely populated province in the Pampas in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. It borders San Luis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, Río Negro, Neuquén and Mendoza, and is larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, Ireland or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Only two major rivers cross it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, on the border with Río Negro, and the Salado, whose level has been dropping as its tributaries in Mendoza are diverted for irrigation.</p><p>The centre and east are a plain tilted gently towards the east, with a calcrete crust and cut by valleys running north-east to south-west. Some of these hold very large fossil inland dunes, and they now lie between the dry and humid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampas</a>. The province is mostly flat but has mountains such as the Sierra de Lihuel Calel, made largely of ignimbrite, a volcanic rock. The climate is temperate in the east and semi-arid in the west, and most rain falls in summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernandarias">Hernando Arias de Saavedra</a> was the first European explorer to reach the area, in 1604, and Jerónimo Luis de Cabrera explored it in 1662. Spanish colonists did not establish permanent settlements until the 18th century. Resistance by the local indigenous people prevented much expansion until the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, and it ended only with Julio Roca's conquest of the desert in the 19th century. The territory was then divided among the officers, who built the first Argentine settlements.</p><p>The Territorio Nacional de La Pampa Central was created in 1884, with about 25,000 inhabitants and with territory that included the Río Negro Province and parts of other surrounding provinces. By 1915 it had 110,000 residents, a sign of the movement of settlers into the area. In 1952 the province's constitution was written and it was renamed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eva-peron">Eva Perón</a>. In 1955, after the government changed and the Peróns went into exile, La Pampa and Chaco, which had been named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a>, took back their original names.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> ranching is the most important agricultural activity and takes place across the province, while the more fertile north-east grows wheat, sunflower, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and other cereals. The province also has oil and gas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and deposits of sodium chloride, sodium sulfate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. Its capital is Santa Rosa, and it is divided into 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. Visitors go to Lihué Calel National Park and the Parque Luro Provincial Reserve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Pampa Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1835 the Mapuche leader Juan Calfucurá crossed from Araucanía to the pampas and seized control of the Salinas Grandes, the great salt flats on the edge of modern <b>La Pampa</b> and Buenos Aires provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/salinas-grandes-confederation">Salinas Grandes Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
